## The migration shock
<!-- claim -->
**{{ v('ch7.message') }}**
<!-- step: scatter -->
In {{ v('policy.migration_years') }} people moved. The shock this chapter measures is domestic migration per hundred residents over those two years less the same measure over {{ v('policy.migration_pre_years') }}, so that a metro which gained people every year is not credited with a pandemic it did not have. Across {{ v('ch7.metros') }} metros the shock runs from {{ v('ch7.shock.p10') }} at the tenth percentile to {{ v('ch7.shock.p90') }} at the ninetieth. Against it the chart plots price growth from {{ v('ch7.window') }}, which averaged {{ v('ch7.growth.mean') }}. The slope is {{ v('ch7.coef') }} per person gained per hundred residents (interval {{ v('ch7.low') }} to {{ v('ch7.high') }}), which between the tenth and the ninetieth percentile metro is {{ v('ch7.effect_p90_vs_p10') }} of price growth. The regression a reader would run first, growth on the pandemic migration level alone with no control, gives {{ v('ch7.naive.coef') }} (interval {{ v('ch7.naive.low') }} to {{ v('ch7.naive.high') }}); it is on the shocks page because the simulator shows what the control removes.
<!-- step: placebo -->
The objection is that the metros that gained people were already on a different path. The placebo asks the same question of the years before the pandemic: growth from {{ v('ch7.placebo_window') }} on the same shock, with the migration of those years as its control. It is {{ v('ch7.placebo.coef') }} (interval {{ v('ch7.placebo.low') }} to {{ v('ch7.placebo.high') }}). The message rule reads the two intervals together and has a branch for every outcome, including the one where the placebo moves.
<!-- step: synthetic -->
For the {{ v('ch7.sc.count') }} metros with the largest shocks, {{ v('ch7.top_gainers') }}, a synthetic control is built from the metros whose shock was small, with every donor searched and then treated in turn as a placebo. {{ v('ch7.sc.1.metro') }} ran {{ v('ch7.sc.1.peak_gap') }} above its synthetic twin at the peak and {{ v('ch7.sc.1.last_gap') }} in the latest quarter, with a permutation p value of {{ v('ch7.sc.1.p') }} over {{ v('ch7.sc.1.donors') }} donors; {{ v('ch7.sc.2.metro') }} ran {{ v('ch7.sc.2.peak_gap') }} at the peak and {{ v('ch7.sc.2.last_gap') }} now ({{ v('ch7.sc.2.p') }}); {{ v('ch7.sc.3.metro') }} ran {{ v('ch7.sc.3.peak_gap') }} and {{ v('ch7.sc.3.last_gap') }} ({{ v('ch7.sc.3.p') }}). The gap after the shock window is the reversal, or its absence, and the chart shows it rather than naming it.
<!-- method -->
Plan `{{ v('ch7.plan_hash') }}`. {{ v('ch7.method.estimator') }}; {{ v('ch7.method.interval') }}; {{ v('ch7.method.split') }}; {{ v('ch7.method.family') }}. On the simulator the controlled estimate recovers the planted response with a mean bias of {{ v('recovery.migration_effect.mean_bias') }} and {{ v('recovery.migration_effect.mean_coverage') }} coverage; the regression on the level alone carries a bias of {{ v('recovery.migration_naive.none.bias') }} with no reverse causality and {{ v('recovery.migration_naive.strong.bias') }} under strong reverse causality; and the placebo flags the strong reverse causality condition in {{ v('recovery.migration.strong.flag_rate') }} of worlds against {{ v('recovery.migration.none.flag_rate') }} when there is none. That last pair is the power of the test, and it is the limit this chapter admits in print.
